<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Sumatra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> on the western coast of Sumatra, with its capital at Padang. It is the homeland of the Minangkabau, a matrilineal Muslim people, and earlier held the centres of the Malayu kingdom of Dharmasraya and the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityawarman">Adityawarman</a>. In the 19th century it was the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padri-war">Padri War</a> between religious reformers, traditional leaders and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-sumatra.jpg" alt="West Sumatra" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Sumatra_in_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> lies in the middle of Sumatra's western coast, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> along a coastline of 375 km, and includes the Mentawai Islands offshore. Plains and the volcanic highlands of the Barisan mountains make up the land, with lakes such as Maninjau and Singkarak, and peaks such as Kerinci at 3,805 m and Marapi at 2,891 m.</p><p>Dense tropical forest shelters Sumatran tigers and Rafflesia arnoldii, the largest flower in the world, and the province holds Siberut National Park and part of Kerinci Seblat National Park. It is known as Bumi Minangkabau, the Land of the Minangkabau, though the traditional Minangkabau region is wider than the province and reaches Negeri Sembilan in Malaysia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the Minangkabau chronicle, the Tambo, many small kingdoms stood in the area between the 1st and 16th centuries, usually under the influence of larger ones such as Malayu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagaruyung">Pagaruyung</a>. The Pagaruyung Kingdom was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityawarman">Adityawarman</a> in 1347. The name Minangkabau is explained by a legend in which locals faced a foreign kingdom, usually taken to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, in a contest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>: a suckling calf tore open the large bull, giving the phrase manang kabau, winning cattle. The first European to reach the region was the French explorer Jean Parmentier, around 1523.</p><p>In the early 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagaruyung">Pagaruyung</a> had to recognise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the Aceh Sultanate. Around 1665 the Minangkabau of the west coast rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>'s governor, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, which wished to end Aceh's monopoly on gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, expelled Aceh from the coast in 1666. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> the region became the residency <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>'s West Coast. During the Japanese occupation it was renamed Sumatora Nishi Kaigan Shu. Before becoming a province in 1957, West Sumatra was part of the province of Central Sumatra, which existed from 1948 to 1957.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital and largest city is Padang, and the province is divided into twelve regencies and seven cities. About 5.5 million people lived there at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, most of them Minangkabau, who speak Minangkabau and are predominantly Muslim, with followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> mainly in the Mentawai Islands. Minangkabau society is matrilineal, and the traditional house is the Rumah Gadang, with its horned roof called gonjong, built with wooden pegs rather than iron nails. Andalas University is the oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Padang cuisine, built on gulai curry, chili and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, is the food of the Minangkabau.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Sumatra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Java War it fought the Padri War in <b>West Sumatra</b> until 1837 and then turned to the outer islands, where most of the archipelago still lay beyond Dutch control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its centre, Dharmasraya, lay in today's <b>West Sumatra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dharmasraya">Dharmasraya</a></li></ul></section>
